164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 212 0 R
/E (The researchers found that wet soil and city surfaces made flooding worse. Earlier storms had already soaked the ground, so about 92% of the rainfall became runoff during the largest flood peak. Roads, roofs, and parking lots also kept water from soaking into the ground, and city structures increased the flooded area by about 30% in the model. The team also found a flood tipping point: once the Schuylkill River rose above about the 100-year flood level, water spread much faster. High tides and future sea-level rise could increase flooding even more.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
